<div class="pt-6 md:pt-12">
  <p class="text-[10px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-3">The thesis · tight</p>
  <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl mb-8">What GTM Radar becomes.</h2>

  <div class="space-y-5 max-w-[640px]">
    <p class="font-serif text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
      A recruiter reads one thing at a time.
    </p>
    <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
      A system reads 55 companies across eight signal types every week and learns which combinations predict which hire — org-shape shifts (sales-to-engineering ratio, tenure distribution skew), departure-prediction (vest cliffs, base-rate tenure, LinkedIn activity), latency-edge events (SEC filings, job posting deltas, pricing-page changes).
    </p>
    <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
      None of the signals are novel — Crunchbase and Carta sell most of them. The edge is correlating them at scale, backtested over 18 months of weekly briefings, until the rubric ranks "most likely to hire" and "most likely to be open" with accuracy a manual recruiter can't match.
    </p>
    <p class="font-serif text-base text-ink/85 leading-relaxed">
      The real shift, though, isn't faster intel on the names a recruiter already knows. It's surfacing the names she doesn't — three weeks before they know they're hiring, and before they show up on anyone else's radar.
    </p>
    <p class="font-serif text-lg text-ink leading-relaxed font-semibold">
      v1 is the four PDFs. v2 is the system. The PDFs are how the rubric gets sharp enough to be the system.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
